#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/ba5c9db77780e1298423e58aaf6f9a54968db34643cfe98bfce96231ab6d3afc.png) \n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/db1215d898d9a9af40907393d15bf2a03d1f709d857925e9756cfe15db2f9a07.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/5998f460a1889d2d938c3d530ef86c37f4a76f746ce0885e7892407aa7a2b02f.png) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184068 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63cdb472-4149-5ee2-99a0-154728b3000a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380555} {:answer/id #uuid "7405db3c-b8fc-5005-ab9f-efc6f40b31f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380556} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6fc5a2-3fc6-5edf-85ca-7c6cfa856d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380557} {:answer/id #uuid "9edefae1-99fe-55e6-996c-b6cc70641d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380558} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee806ea-24a1-53da-ad51-7ddab977ad4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ee806ea-24a1-53da-ad51-7ddab977ad4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380559} :answer-field/id #uuid "64372918-a34b-53d3-ab95-e990a67e53a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3252368f-4a55-5037-b3b9-3787d2c9c3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-141220" :question/problem "Consider a $8\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =7 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =7-2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] 