 & =10-6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d67f50b-b895-5cbd-a6a1-ac4f0cdcbfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380569} {:answer/id #uuid "4abf48fb-e24f-5297-9322-353c8e378781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380570} {:answer/id #uuid "fb32f28f-3191-5af2-ba09-4c5e4e3219ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380571} {:answer/id #uuid "57046145-462d-5860-bd8c-a203d0772b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea48e32-f494-5d86-930f-5f4e0db40fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57046145-462d-5860-bd8c-a203d0772b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572} :answer-field/id #uuid "d669704f-b5e1-579e-a26f-1aeac6c5b88a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8b785a4-1b36-5c95-bda1-10b7c61088fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141228" :question/problem "Consider a $6\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0baad3-bb60-54f4-8d39-a06ccd11db08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380576} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b7428e-b2e8-5bd4-b718-599cefd37382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380577} {:answer/id #uuid "83e426f4-a835-57e3-a9f0-eb7409efeff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380578} {:answer/id #uuid "446785ba-b09d-5a38-a465-1854412cac83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380579} {:answer/id #uuid "00a523cd-01dd-50ce-8953-4259c1cfd022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d0baad3-bb60-54f4-8d39-a06ccd11db08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380576} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dee7666-36f2-5d4c-9901-7ecbbe9ba425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74c4aa98-5658-5936-9578-3c3dafe878a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141230" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7,$ and since $B$ is formed by $4$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3adaf5-4225-5c05-a7f9-436d62937258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380583} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb208c-2d76-5449-94e3-ab3e50293c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380584} {:answer/id #uuid "52d2a389-da14-5bed-b6b0-532031630d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380585} {:answer/id #uuid "7708579b-f30b-5036-92a5-aa1cdeb1e0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380586} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c8812-45ee-56db-b4cf-555c1bfb39e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7708579b-f30b-5036-92a5-aa1cdeb1e0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380586} :answer-field/id #uuid "039bb6c9-7b60-5d62-9122-3cfd63275c68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6088fbd5-0815-51a5-834a-f9d302fbb385" :question/math-academy-id "q-142480" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =6 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a834cae2-6d81-5fb7-9d76-2f3a8d84382c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380590} {:answer/id #uuid "54961682-e9aa-54c3-bf71-21adb57e8b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380591} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ef5b21-8a4f-5e2a-b2db-428fe8bd6851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380592} {:answer/id #uuid "9678d726-2a3d-5127-aa8c-d4fb8482379f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380593} {:answer/id #uuid "3a49a729-b324-5a87-9009-9b9eacd0cd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ef5b21-8a4f-5e2a-b2db-428fe8bd6851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380592} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f43779-695f-5e5e-ac53-a18f94571131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ceb32f9-2e8d-5cce-8970-94cdebdcdbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-142486" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8465f0fc-cb7c-5d6b-bcb3-e57968cc582f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380597} {:answer/id #uuid "7c63647f-3f27-5317-b3c7-9ee5cee9eace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380598} {:answer/id #uuid "1a50727b-68d5-516b-813c-30147d4e7f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380599} {:answer/id #uuid "9c964c65-f97c-5abd-94f0-bf9bf59d7c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380600} {:answer/id #uuid "5b170c5c-6151-589b-a493-43d082a73ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8465f0fc-cb7c-5d6b-bcb3-e57968cc582f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380597} :answer-field/id #uuid "e023e1c0-dafe-520f-9db8-1da79e499e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2af6f312-d668-5544-bc66-0a068fde3eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-142487" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $6\\times 8$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4},{b}_{5}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8,$ and since $B$ is formed by $5$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =8 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =8-5 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363ec4c8-d6a0-5aa6-ad00-c9a687948475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380604} {:answer/id #uuid "a009f88b-6dc3-51f9-b38f-a7a36d820480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380605} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a5693f-526f-5d89-b4b6-44610d4ccabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380606} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6b3f55-1b43-571b-9754-ef7e7b502c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380607} {:answer/id #uuid "3366cedf-9a5c-58fe-9b7b-c20aa3be1cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef6b3f55-1b43-571b-9754-ef7e7b502c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380607} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c06cc56-d025-542e-96d9-cf3836bc8063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6011c5e7-8c57-59d8-856d-f61a86c3b068" :question/math-academy-id "q-142544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1fa245b-fb54-51cf-947b-160d5324dfa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380611} {:answer/id #uuid "4554cd0b-0f37-557d-a395-1b5ad8e2aee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380612} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d19593-9e96-5af5-9afd-e29692597694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380613} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc6550e-d21f-5e29-bb17-a0d6f831d75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380614} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b9c7a-3c30-5f5a-ac74-02a7575702e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d19593-9e96-5af5-9afd-e29692597694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380613} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa83d57-eb1f-5de1-979b-4f69adc7bb39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af7c4423-80cf-5f04-bb94-54a129eabb62" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321f7c25-d9fb-5897-9ead-f605e953ca9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380618} {:answer/id #uuid "98b6b0e9-8192-5082-9e63-4f9a0927f8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380619} {:answer/id #uuid "0499ea4a-49ab-5b76-9e44-f469aef7a64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380620} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbca8ca-b281-54fd-995f-d541ee84fdfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380621} {:answer/id #uuid "921c7c05-e1e9-59ff-a7af-aaa257e9ca88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b6b0e9-8192-5082-9e63-4f9a0927f8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380619} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d55df8c-9022-5b6d-8229-28d1f821bca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a46765c0-748f-5736-84db-b7439678cd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-35305" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6645d403-d8f7-59f4-a57c-95bd64d4798d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380625} {:answer/id #uuid "87cf0230-22d3-5bab-8ed0-83c69992869a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380626} {:answer/id #uuid "512f5941-e2f8-5e42-9ede-9ceefe145cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380627} {:answer/id #uuid "0c55fc6e-5a5e-546f-baed-a519f3344aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380628} {:answer/id #uuid "972bb9cd-5952-5a01-8ea7-927e2650e383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "512f5941-e2f8-5e42-9ede-9ceefe145cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380627} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4625d74-1fce-5a9f-9462-cb76b079902c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f11432d-0236-541d-88c7-498a5f6d2929" :question/math-academy-id "q-35434" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41b70a84-612b-501f-9ade-9ce2a414e56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380632} {:answer/id #uuid "30ce3811-4af9-5bfa-87b0-e08c7b34c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380633} {:answer/id #uuid "acf1a6fc-e8ff-504d-a8b3-1de785cafe38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380634} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9e7813-ec4f-5ec6-8427-6f5a34fd035e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380635} {:answer/id #uuid "892b2d9a-d6ff-5201-acba-2390976a7ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf1a6fc-e8ff-504d-a8b3-1de785cafe38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380634} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e67cac8-ed4c-5c1e-884d-05fec5292a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40b2a8dc-ab66-5526-b7b4-c5877b54014f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79353" :question/problem "Consider a $7\\times 9$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=7$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 7+\\text{nullity}(A) & =9 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =9-7 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f738c2fe-b2a7-5fab-bc25-4d0425020ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380639} {:answer/id #uuid "738cba16-c53f-5666-8bc3-b10b76db3013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380640} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8d88f5-0880-5586-994a-101bd903912b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380641} {:answer/id #uuid "65b26651-f1dd-5517-b09f-81d83eb81c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380642} {:answer/id #uuid "269f3887-6e0d-5a8e-838f-f4eb5a94181b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738cba16-c53f-5666-8bc3-b10b76db3013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380640} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7daef0-2c15-5e93-99b2-4a439fa1efed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac20d344-81cf-56d4-bf30-c4e9af2d3945" :question/math-academy-id "q-79417" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f032b2d-6c34-57d2-b972-b5549065832b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380646} {:answer/id #uuid "97616f3f-c18e-5465-baac-9478b7e86517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380647} {:answer/id #uuid "9c076e81-b957-5d04-b15a-c58ffdb14cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380648} {:answer/id #uuid "8b49e5c0-d706-5125-bb5d-a61c435445e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380649} {:answer/id #uuid "a185966c-c330-505f-bf04-afe06c599793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f032b2d-6c34-57d2-b972-b5549065832b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380646} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c4cc3b2-fc5b-5ffe-af02-22c8cff4517f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64441ddf-4fa5-5744-bddd-e09fbb9ec953" :question/math-academy-id "q-79420" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is not  invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "778e78a0-1e7b-5be5-9826-880e489a222d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380653} {:answer/id #uuid "cafda0bf-ad24-5678-b34b-df4024f13245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380654} {:answer/id #uuid "721e6894-066d-5f48-8fe0-2e3e783efdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380655} {:answer/id #uuid "71aa4e6d-eb5c-5f79-ae63-fd84755ce8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380656} {:answer/id #uuid "8719b067-4fc7-5cdc-9095-9c8b32baeb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8719b067-4fc7-5cdc-9095-9c8b32baeb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657} :answer-field/id #uuid "af233a0c-f575-5acb-a21f-3aefad463c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ca10a0-33db-5c54-bbcd-e87f1a964f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-79423" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n- $A$ is **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
